1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,150
A tent standing in the wind.

2
00:00:02,150 --> 00:00:03,106
A rowing boat.

3
00:00:03,106 --> 00:00:04,104
A busy factory.

4
00:00:04,504 --> 00:00:05,780
Aarav caught a cold.

5
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:09,661
All night he coughed while he studied,
bringing up sticky mucus from his

6
00:00:09,661 --> 00:00:10,561
windpipe.

7
00:00:10,762 --> 00:00:14,740
His doctor said tiny hairs on the
cells of his windpipe sweep mucus and

8
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:15,896
dust up and out.

9
00:00:15,896 --> 00:00:19,461
Aarav asked: how can a hair
on a single cell move at all?

10
00:00:19,811 --> 00:00:23,439
The answer is a skeleton inside
the cell, made of protein tubes.

11
00:00:23,439 --> 00:00:26,352
The same tubes build cilia,
flagella and centrioles.

12
00:00:26,352 --> 00:00:29,139
And the proteins themselves
are built by ribosomes.

13
00:00:29,639 --> 00:00:34,543
By the end, you will know ribosome
sizes, see the nine plus two pattern,

14
00:00:34,543 --> 00:00:36,489
and see inside a centriole.

15
00:00:37,139 --> 00:00:38,999
First, the protein builders.

16
00:00:39,349 --> 00:00:40,855
Ribosomes are tiny grains.

17
00:00:40,855 --> 00:00:44,869
George Palade first saw them as dense
particles under the electron microscope,

18
00:00:44,869 --> 00:00:45,769
in 1953.

19
00:00:45,967 --> 00:00:49,664
They are made of RNA and proteins,
and they have no membrane around them.

20
00:00:49,664 --> 00:00:51,314
Their job is to build proteins.

21
00:00:51,764 --> 00:00:54,232
Every ribosome has two
parts, called subunits.

22
00:00:54,232 --> 00:00:58,163
In eukaryotic cells, ribosomes are
80S, made of a 60S and a 40S subunit.

23
00:00:58,163 --> 00:01:01,933
In prokaryotic cells, they are 70S,
made of a 50S and a 30S subunit.

24
00:01:02,383 --> 00:01:04,386
S stands for the Svedberg unit.

25
00:01:04,386 --> 00:01:08,261
It tells how fast a particle
settles when it is spun very fast.

26
00:01:08,261 --> 00:01:10,801
That is a clue to its size and density.

27
00:01:11,451 --> 00:01:15,096
Now let's zoom into the cell's
skeleton, one level at a time.

28
00:01:15,446 --> 00:01:20,050
Inside the cytoplasm runs a network of
protein threads, called the cytoskeleton.

29
00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:23,611
It has microtubules, microfilaments
and intermediate filaments.

30
00:01:23,611 --> 00:01:27,298
It gives support, keeps the
cell's shape, and helps it move.

31
00:01:27,798 --> 00:01:29,933
Zoom to the edge of a windpipe cell.

32
00:01:29,933 --> 00:01:32,701
Hair-like outgrowths of the
cell membrane stick out.

33
00:01:32,701 --> 00:01:33,739
These are cilia.

34
00:01:34,239 --> 00:01:35,694
Cut one cilium across.

35
00:01:35,694 --> 00:01:37,999
It is covered by the plasma membrane.

36
00:01:37,999 --> 00:01:41,997
Its core is called the axoneme, made
of microtubules running along its

37
00:01:41,997 --> 00:01:42,897
length.

38
00:01:43,101 --> 00:01:44,502
Zoom into the axoneme.

39
00:01:44,502 --> 00:01:47,895
Nine pairs of microtubules sit
in a ring, called doublets.

40
00:01:47,895 --> 00:01:50,336
Two single microtubules sit in the centre.

41
00:01:50,336 --> 00:01:52,337
This is the nine plus two array.

42
00:01:52,337 --> 00:01:56,505
Radial spokes join the centre to each
doublet, and linkers join the doublets

43
00:01:56,505 --> 00:01:57,501
to each other.

44
00:01:58,151 --> 00:02:01,466
Now watch how cilia
move, one step at a time.

45
00:02:01,816 --> 00:02:02,716
Step one.

46
00:02:02,617 --> 00:02:06,729
A cilium stiffens and sweeps
forward, like an oar pushing water.

47
00:02:07,479 --> 00:02:08,379
Step two.

48
00:02:08,238 --> 00:02:12,780
It bends and swings back, close to the
cell, so it does not push the fluid

49
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:13,680
back.

50
00:02:14,048 --> 00:02:14,948
Step three.

51
00:02:14,905 --> 00:02:19,070
Neighbouring cilia beat one after
another, like a wave in a stadium.

52
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:20,720
Step four.

53
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:25,323
The fluid above them, with mucus and
dust, moves along in one direction.

54
00:02:26,073 --> 00:02:26,973
Step five.

55
00:02:26,927 --> 00:02:28,487
A flagellum is longer.

56
00:02:28,487 --> 00:02:31,166
It whips to push the whole cell forward.

57
00:02:31,166 --> 00:02:35,345
Both cilia and flagella grow from a
centriole-like structure, called a

58
00:02:35,345 --> 00:02:36,248
basal body.

59
00:02:36,998 --> 00:02:38,106
Now the centrosome.

60
00:02:38,106 --> 00:02:41,766
It is an organelle that usually holds
two cylinders, called centrioles.

61
00:02:42,116 --> 00:02:44,840
The two centrioles lie at
right angles to each other.

62
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:48,266
Around them is a shapeless cloud,
called the pericentriolar material.

63
00:02:48,766 --> 00:02:52,064
Cut a centriole across and
it looks like a cartwheel.

64
00:02:52,064 --> 00:02:56,278
It has nine evenly spaced fibrils,
made of a protein called tubulin.

65
00:02:56,278 --> 00:02:59,963
Each fibril is a triplet, three
tubes joined side by side.

66
00:03:00,463 --> 00:03:04,764
In the centre is a protein hub, joined
to the triplets by radial spokes.

67
00:03:05,264 --> 00:03:06,772
Centrioles have two jobs.

68
00:03:06,772 --> 00:03:09,580
They form the basal bodies
of cilia and flagella.

69
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:13,992
And in animal cells, they form the
spindle fibres, used when a cell divides.

70
00:03:14,642 --> 00:03:15,778
Side by side.

71
00:03:15,778 --> 00:03:16,678
Size.

72
00:03:16,368 --> 00:03:18,262
Cilia are small and many.

73
00:03:18,262 --> 00:03:20,419
Flagella are longer and fewer.

74
00:03:21,169 --> 00:03:22,069
The job.

75
00:03:21,929 --> 00:03:25,998
Cilia work like oars, moving
the cell or the fluid around it.

76
00:03:25,998 --> 00:03:27,973
Flagella push the cell along.

77
00:03:28,723 --> 00:03:29,623
Inside.

78
00:03:29,378 --> 00:03:34,292
Both have the same nine plus two
axoneme, and both grow from a basal body.

79
00:03:34,292 --> 00:03:38,252
Bacteria also have flagella, but
theirs are built differently.

80
00:03:39,002 --> 00:03:39,902
Your turn.

81
00:03:39,759 --> 00:03:42,540
A cilium has nine pairs
plus two in the centre.

82
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:44,322
A centriole has nine triplets.

83
00:03:44,322 --> 00:03:46,686
Which one has microtubules in its centre?

84
00:03:46,686 --> 00:03:47,799
Pause and decide.

85
00:03:50,999 --> 00:03:51,899
The cilium!

86
00:03:51,784 --> 00:03:55,291
A centriole has a protein hub
in the centre, not microtubules.

87
00:03:55,941 --> 00:03:56,841
Remember this.

88
00:03:56,781 --> 00:03:58,475
Cilia have nine pairs plus two.

89
00:03:58,475 --> 00:04:00,570
Centrioles have nine triplets and a hub.

90
00:04:01,170 --> 00:04:02,766
Now, a worked example.

91
00:04:02,766 --> 00:04:04,607
Six places in living things.

92
00:04:04,607 --> 00:04:06,458
Is the ribosome 80S or 70S?

93
00:04:06,808 --> 00:04:08,984
The cytoplasm of a liver cell.

94
00:04:08,984 --> 00:04:10,510
Eukaryotic, so 80S.

95
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:11,860
A bacterium.

96
00:04:11,759 --> 00:04:13,021
Prokaryotic, so 70S.

97
00:04:13,471 --> 00:04:14,754
Inside a mitochondrion.

98
00:04:14,754 --> 00:04:15,654
70S.

99
00:04:15,619 --> 00:04:17,721
The rough ER of an onion root cell.

100
00:04:17,721 --> 00:04:18,621
80S.

101
00:04:18,603 --> 00:04:19,834
Inside a chloroplast.

102
00:04:19,834 --> 00:04:20,734
70S.

103
00:04:20,708 --> 00:04:23,127
And the cytoplasm of a windpipe cell.

104
00:04:23,127 --> 00:04:24,027
80S.

105
00:04:24,267 --> 00:04:25,728
Now, the exam trap.

106
00:04:26,078 --> 00:04:28,173
Many students think the
two subunits must add up.

107
00:04:28,173 --> 00:04:29,537
60S plus 40S should make 100S.

108
00:04:29,937 --> 00:04:33,795
But S measures how fast a
particle settles, not its weight.

109
00:04:33,795 --> 00:04:35,802
The values do not simply add.

110
00:04:35,802 --> 00:04:38,199
The whole eukaryotic ribosome is 80S.

111
00:04:38,849 --> 00:04:40,280
Where is this asked?

112
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:44,736
In board papers, as the nine plus
two diagram or ribosome types, and in

113
00:04:44,736 --> 00:04:46,696
NEET, as statement questions.

114
00:04:47,296 --> 00:04:48,443
Exam level now.

115
00:04:48,443 --> 00:04:50,231
Which statement is correct?

116
00:04:50,231 --> 00:04:51,427
Pause and solve.

117
00:04:56,827 --> 00:04:58,163
The answer is C.

118
00:04:58,663 --> 00:05:01,253
The axoneme has nine
doublets, not triplets.

119
00:05:01,253 --> 00:05:04,618
The two centrioles lie at
right angles, not side by side.

120
00:05:05,118 --> 00:05:07,286
And ribosomes have no membrane at all.

121
00:05:07,286 --> 00:05:08,458
Only C is correct.

122
00:05:09,058 --> 00:05:10,598
Here is your recap.

123
00:05:10,598 --> 00:05:12,181
Screenshot this card.

124
00:05:15,381 --> 00:05:17,567
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

125
00:05:17,967 --> 00:05:18,867
One.

126
00:05:18,306 --> 00:05:20,325
What are the two subunits
of an 80S ribosome?

127
00:05:21,225 --> 00:05:22,125
Two.

128
00:05:21,626 --> 00:05:23,410
What is the nine plus two array?

129
00:05:24,310 --> 00:05:25,210
Three.

130
00:05:24,697 --> 00:05:25,933
Name two jobs of centrioles.

131
00:05:27,033 --> 00:05:31,105
Next time: how does two metres
of DNA fit inside one tiny cell?

132
00:05:31,105 --> 00:05:33,845
We find out in the
nucleus and chromosomes.
